; pi4DisplayProbe.pi4 - does display.pi4 actually get a framebuffer, and
;                       does its geometry arithmetic agree with the
;                       numbers the firmware chose?
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship. (House rule 19.)
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHY THIS PROGRAM EXISTS
; ======================================================================
; display.pi4 was written complete and then deliberately disabled. Its
; seven VideoCore framebuffer tag numbers were left as EMPTY SLOTS and
; DisplayInit() refused with #DSP_ENOTAGS, because the defining kernel
; header was not on this machine and the file would not guess.
;
; That header arrived on 2026-08-25: the Linux kernel's
; include/soc/bcm2835/raspberrypi-firmware.h. mailbox.pi4 and this file
; cite it as "Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h", the name of the
; reference copy that was read; the file itself is not part of this
; repository. tools/a64/a64_display_check.py pins the tag values it
; needs, with the header line each one came from.
;
; All the values live in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4 as #MBX_TAG_FB_*.
; They were re-checked against the header, value by value, while this
; probe was written; every one matches:
;
;   FRAMEBUFFER_ALLOCATE                  $00040001
;   FRAMEBUFFER_GET_PHYSICAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT $00040003
;   FRAMEBUFFER_GET_PITCH                 $00040008
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_PHYSICAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT $00048003
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_VIRTUAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT  $00048004
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_DEPTH                 $00048005
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_PIXEL_ORDER           $00048006
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_ALPHA_MODE            $00048007
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_VIRTUAL_OFFSET        $00048009
;   FRAMEBUFFER_SET_OVERSCAN              $0004800A
;
; TEN SLOTS SINCE 2026-08-26, AND DisplayStandardTags() FILLS ALL TEN.
; Three of them - get-physical, set-alpha-mode, set-overscan - arrived
; with the rewrite that put nine tags in one mailbox message, and the
; set-pixel-order slot that section 3 used to assert must stay EMPTY is
; now asserted to be FILLED. That reversal is not tidying and it is
; argued where the assertion is, in section 3; the short version is that
; the meaning of the pixel-order value stopped being uncitable the same
; day, and that a tag left out of a combined message is not a default
; left alone but a gap in the state the firmware allocates against.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT THIS PROBE WAS GREEN THROUGH, AND WHAT IT NOW CHECKS
; ======================================================================
; This program and its harness passed, 39 checks out of 39, over a
; library that produced a 2 x 2 FRAMEBUFFER on real silicon. Booted
; through U-Boot the seven-message bring-up worked; booted straight from
; the firmware it returned success over a surface two pixels square in
; front of a 1920 x 1080 panel, and the console built on it computed a
; negative number of rows.
;
; Nothing in the checks below was wrong. The gap was that they were all
; downstream of a mailbox model that answered separate messages out of
; state accumulated across all of them - and the real firmware
; accumulates within ONE transaction and allocates against that. So the
; failure was invisible to every assertion in this file, because every
; one of them starts from the geometry DisplayInit() came back with.
;
; What is new here is section 3's tag assertions and, in the harness,
; the shape of the traffic: how many messages, which tags in each, in
; what order, carrying what words. That is the only place the defect was
; ever visible, and it is the reason the harness watches the wire
; instead of only reading the pixels.
;
; So the library may be complete and working, and nothing has ever
; called it. This program calls it, and reports what happens rather
; than assuming.
;
; ======================================================================
;  BUILD IT EXACTLY LIKE THIS
; ======================================================================
;   pmfc RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DisplayProbe.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o pi4DisplayProbe.img
;
; --entry-returns is why this is safe to send to a resident Anvil:
; Main() finishing RETURNS to the monitor with the verdict in x0 instead
; of trapping forever, so a wrong answer costs a line of output and not
; a walk to the plug.
;
; The load address must stay in the LOW GIGABYTE. mailbox.pi4 refuses
; with #MBX_ERR_ADDRESS if its buffer lands at or above $40000000,
; because the dma-ranges alias the VideoCore is named through
; (bcm2711.dtsi:45) is only $40000000 long. 0x400000 is well inside it.
;
; ======================================================================
;  IT RUNS WITHOUT HARDWARE TOO
; ======================================================================
; tools/a64/a64_display_check.py builds this exact file and executes the
; exact image on the project's A64 oracle (tools/a64/a64_interp.py),
; against a modelled PL011 and a modelled VideoCore that CLAMPS the mode
; and returns a PADDED pitch. The whole serial log below is produced
; there before the board ever sees it, and every pixel is checked in the
; harness at addresses the harness computes for itself.
;
; ======================================================================
;  NO MONITOR IS ATTACHED, AND THAT DOES NOT STOP MOST OF THIS
; ======================================================================
; A framebuffer is DRAM. Every pixel written can be read back, so the
; geometry arithmetic can be PROVEN against the real pitch the firmware
; chose without anything being plugged into the HDMI socket. What cannot
; be settled without a screen is which channel is which and whether an
; image appears at all. Each section says which kind it is, and the
; summary at the end lists the open questions in one place.
;
; The one question a missing monitor is itself the experiment for:
; DOES THE FIRMWARE ALLOCATE A FRAMEBUFFER WITH HDMI UNPLUGGED? It may
; refuse, it may clamp to a default mode, or it may hand back exactly
; what was asked for. Section 4 reports which, and that is a result
; either way.
;
; WHICH IS ALSO WHY NOTHING BELOW HARDCODES A COORDINATE. An early draft
; drew its rectangles at x=100..800 and put the console at (100,400)
; 1200x400, which is a picture on a 1920x1080 surface and almost nothing
; on the 640x480 the firmware may well clamp to with the cable out. The
; layout is computed from the width and height the firmware ACTUALLY
; gave, printed before it is used, and the assertions read the same
; variables - so the probe draws a proportionally identical picture at
; any mode, and says which one it drew.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE TWO PREDICTED FAILURES, STATED BEFORE THE RUN
; ======================================================================
;   1. PITCH. The firmware picks the bytes-per-row and it is not
;      obliged to be width*bpp. Getting it wrong does not blank the
;      screen, it SHEARS the picture diagonally. Section 7 writes single
;      pixels at chosen coordinates and reads the raw bytes back at
;      base + y*pitch, and separately checks that base + y*width*bpp is
;      still background - which is the shear, asserted rather than
;      reasoned about. If the firmware happens to return a pitch equal
;      to width*bpp that control is vacuous, and the section says so
;      instead of quietly claiming a pass.
;   2. RED AND BLUE SWAPPED. Only an eye can settle this.
;      DisplayColourSwap(1) is the fix and it needs no firmware tag.
;      Section 9 draws pure red, green and blue at stated coordinates
;      and PRINTS THE PIXEL WORD it wrote, so that whoever does plug a
;      monitor in can compare what they see against a number rather
;      than against a memory.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT THIS PROGRAM REFUSES TO DO
; ======================================================================
; It does not draw one pixel until it has checked that the framebuffer
; the firmware handed back lies OUTSIDE the low 64 MiB. The monitor, this
; image at $400000 and its stack at $3000000 all live down there, and a
; bad base address would not produce a graphical glitch - it would
; overwrite the program that is running. See PrRegionSafe().
;
; ======================================================================
;  RECORDED AND REJECTED
; ======================================================================
; * Calling DisplayInit() without DisplayStandardTags() first, to "let
;   the defaults work". There are no defaults; that is the whole design
;   of the slot mechanism. Section 2 makes the refusal an assertion
;   instead - it checks that DisplayInit() DOES refuse with #DSP_ENOTAGS
;   before the tags are filled, so the guard is proven live rather than
;   assumed to have been removed cleanly.
; * Checking the drawn glyph against three pixel coordinates picked by
;   eye from a font dump. Section 10 compares ALL 128 pixels of a cell
;   against the bit the font table actually holds, read here with PeekA.
;   Three coordinates would have tested three coordinates; the table
;   comparison tests the MSB-first bit order, the glyph stride, the row
;   stride and the run loop at once, and it cannot go stale if the font
;   is regenerated.
; * Reading the surface back through DisplayGetPixel() for the geometry
;   claims. Checking display.pi4's arithmetic with display.pi4's
;   arithmetic proves only that it agrees with itself. PrRaw32() below
;   recomputes the address here, from the pitch, and PeekNs it.
;   DisplayGetPixel() is used only where IT is the thing under test.
; * Its own PL011 driver. RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4 exists, but bringing
;   it in would make this probe depend on a second library being correct
;   before it can report that the first one is not. The two registers
;   below are the same two pi4MailboxProbe.pi4 uses, for the same
;   reason, and are cited there.
; * Its own read of CNTPCT_EL0 for the cache timing in section 8.
;   mailbox.pi4 already exposes MbxTicks(), it is already linked in, and
;   house rule 9 says a library beats an intrinsic even when the
;   intrinsic is two words. timer.pi4's Ticks() would have been the
;   other candidate and would have meant a third include.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
; #DSP_DMA_LINKED - MANDATORY, and it must be defined BEFORE display.pi4.
; 0 = the CPU drawing path and nothing else; every procedure that would
; touch the blitter is skipped at the token level and never parsed, and
; what display.pi4 still carries is ten accessors totalling 660 bytes so
; that a program can ask whether the engine was linked and be told no.
; 1 would additionally require RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4 to be included
; ahead of display.pi4.
; display.pi4's THE DMA SEAM section has the reasoning; there is no
; default because a default in the library and a different value here
; would be a hard "Constant already defined" error.
#DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"

; The PL011 the console is already running on. Cited in
; pi4MailboxProbe.pi4 and in RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4; bus $7E201000
; through bcm2711.dtsi:41's soc ranges is ARM $FE201000.
#UART_DR = $FE201000
#UART_FR = $FE201018
#UART_FR_TXFF = 32             ; bit 5, transmit FIFO full

; The floor the framebuffer must sit above before this program will
; write to it. The monitor, this image ($400000) and its stack
; ($3000000) are all below $4000000. See PrRegionSafe().
#SAFE_FLOOR = $4000000

; Below this the drawing sections have nothing to draw in and would
; report failures that are about the mode, not about the library.
#MIN_W = 64
#MIN_H = 48

Global prFails.i
Global prChecks.i

; ======================================================================
;  UART - the same shape as pi4MailboxProbe.pi4
; ======================================================================

Procedure PutC(c.i)
  Define f.i
  Repeat
    f = PeekN(#UART_FR)
  Until (f & #UART_FR_TXFF) = 0
  PokeL(#UART_DR, c)
EndProcedure

; PeekA, not PeekB. Ruled 2026-08-25: PeekB names the SIGNED .b and
; sign-extends on every backend now, so a byte above 127 comes back
; negative and this loop would never meet its terminator the way it
; expects. PeekA is the raw-byte accessor.
Procedure PutS(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekA(*s + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PutC(13) : PutC(10)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    ; Parenthesised: & binds TIGHTER than >> on this compiler, so
    ; "v >> i * 4 & $F" would shift by (i*4 & $F).
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      PutC(48 + n)
    Else
      PutC(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define started.i
  Define digit.i
  If v < 0
    PutC(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  If v = 0
    PutC(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  d = 1000000000000000000
  started = 0
  While d > 0
    digit = (v / d) % 10
    If digit <> 0 Or started = 1
      PutC(48 + digit)
      started = 1
    EndIf
    d = d / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

; "  name = 1234   ($000004D2)"
Procedure Row(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS(" = ")
  PutDec(v)
  PutS("   ($")
  PutHex(v, 8)
  PutS(")")
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; A 64-bit address wants all sixteen digits; folding one to eight is how
; a truncated pointer hides.
Procedure RowAddr(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS(" = $")
  PutHex(v, 16)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  ASSERTIONS
;
;  Every one prints its own line whether it passes or fails, because a
;  diagnostic that only speaks up when something is wrong cannot be
;  distinguished from one that did not run.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i Chk(*name, got.i, want.i)
  prChecks = prChecks + 1
  If got = want
    PutS("  ok   ")
    PutS(*name)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  prFails = prFails + 1
  PutS("  FAIL ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS("   got $")
  PutHex(got, 16)
  PutS("  want $")
  PutHex(want, 16)
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  SAFETY - do not draw into the program that is drawing
; ======================================================================

; PrRegionSafe - 1 if [base, base+size) is somewhere it is safe to
; write. A framebuffer base of zero, or one pointing into the low 64 MiB
; where the monitor and this image live, is not a graphical problem: a
; DisplayClear() over it overwrites the running program and the board
; goes quiet with no message. DisplayInit() already refuses a zero base
; and a size below pitch*height; this is the check it cannot make,
; because a library does not know where its caller was loaded.
Procedure.i PrRegionSafe(base.i, size.i)
  If base < #SAFE_FLOOR
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If size < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The bus->ARM conversion is & $3FFFFFFF, so an ARM address out of it
  ; can never exceed $3FFFFFFF. Anything past that means the conversion
  ; did something other than what display.pi4's header describes.
  If (base + size) > $40000000
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  READBACK HELPERS
;
;  PrRaw32 reaches the surface at an address THIS FILE computes,
;  deliberately not through DisplayGetPixel(). See RECORDED AND REJECTED.
; ======================================================================

; The 32-bit word at (x, y) assuming a row stride of `stride` bytes.
; Masked because PeekN of a pixel with the top alpha byte at $FF has
; bit 31 set and would come back negative into a 64-bit .i.
Procedure.i PrRaw32(base.i, stride.i, x.i, y.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN((base + (y * stride)) + (x * 4)) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PrIsNot - 1 if the two differ. Exists so the shear checks below can
; assert "the pixel is NOT here" without writing what it IS.
;
; THIS IS NOT STYLE. The first version of section 7 asserted that the
; tight-stride address held ZERO, and two of its checks failed against a
; library that was working perfectly. The background after
; DisplayClear(black) is not zero: DisplayRGB() puts $FF in the top byte
; - correct under ARGB, ignored under XRGB - so a cleared pixel reads
; $FF000000. Worse, the one shear check that PASSED passed by accident,
; because at row 1 the tight-stride address happens to land in the row
; padding, which really is untouched zero. A check that is right for the
; wrong reason next to a check that is wrong for the right reason is not
; a section anybody should keep.
;
; Asking "is the magic value here?" removes the whole question. It does
; not care what the background is, it does not care whether the address
; lands in a pixel or in padding, and it says exactly the thing the
; shear bug would make false.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PrIsNot(a.i, b.i)
  If a = b
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; How many pixels in a rectangle differ from `bg`. A count is the
; monitor-free answer to "did the text draw": a cell that rendered has
; some ink and some ground, a cell that rendered nothing has none of one
; of them.
Procedure.i PrInkCount(base.i, stride.i, x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, bg.i)
  Define r.i
  Define c.i
  Define n.i
  n = 0
  r = 0
  While r < h
    c = 0
    While c < w
      If PrRaw32(base, stride, x + c, y + r) <> bg
        n = n + 1
      EndIf
      c = c + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PrGlyphErrors - compare EVERY pixel of a drawn 8x16 cell against the
; bit the font table actually holds. Returns the number that disagree.
;
; This is the check that covers the four things a character renderer can
; get wrong independently: the MSB-of-the-first-byte-is-the-leftmost-
; pixel order, the 16-byte glyph stride, the row stride inside a glyph,
; and the run-coalescing loop in DisplayChar(). Three hand-picked
; coordinates would have covered one of them by luck.
;
; The table is reached as ?dsp_font8x16 + (ch-32)*16, which is
; DisplayFontDefault()'s own layout: fixed 8x16 cells, ASCII 32..126,
; one byte per row. Read with PeekA, never Read/Restore - "Read strides
; 2 bytes whatever the element width" is an open bug on every target
; (Bugs/Open bugs.md:309, open since 2026-08-08) and display.pi4's own
; header records the same avoidance for the same reason.
;
; Scale must be 1 and the cell must be fully on the surface; the caller
; checks both.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PrGlyphErrors(base.i, stride.i, x.i, y.i, ch.i, fg.i, bg.i)
  Define r.i
  Define c.i
  Define b.i
  Define want.i
  Define bad.i
  Define rowbase.i
  bad = 0
  rowbase = ?dsp_font8x16 + ((ch - 32) * 16)
  r = 0
  While r < 16
    b = PeekA(rowbase + r)
    c = 0
    While c < 8
      ; Parenthesised: & binds tighter than >>, so "b & 128 >> c" is
      ; "(b & 128) >> c" - a different, and wrong, question.
      If (b & (128 >> c)) <> 0
        want = fg
      Else
        want = bg
      EndIf
      If PrRaw32(base, stride, x + c, y + r) <> want
        bad = bad + 1
      EndIf
      c = c + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn bad
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  MAIN
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i Main()
  Define rc.i
  Define bus.i
  Define base.i
  Define size.i
  Define w.i
  Define h.i
  Define depth.i
  Define bpp.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define tight.i
  Define pad.i
  Define black.i
  Define red.i
  Define green.i
  Define blue.i
  Define white.i
  Define field.i
  Define magic.i
  Define t0.i
  Define t1.i
  Define t2.i
  Define t3.i
  Define ink.i
  Define cw.i
  Define chh.i
  ; the computed layout - see WHICH IS ALSO WHY NOTHING BELOW HARDCODES
  Define rx.i
  Define ry.i
  Define rw.i
  Define rh.i
  Define gap.i
  Define conx.i
  Define cony.i
  Define conw.i
  Define conh.i
  Define gx.i
  Define gy.i
  Define ncell.i

  prFails = 0
  prChecks = 0

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== Pi 4 display probe - first call ever made into display.pi4 ===")
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  1. THE TRANSPORT
  ;
  ;  display.pi4 calls MailboxInit() itself if it has to, but doing it
  ;  here separates "the mailbox is broken" from "the framebuffer tags
  ;  are wrong" - two failures that would otherwise arrive as the same
  ;  #DSP_ENOMAILBOX.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 1. mailbox transport")
  PutNl()
  If MailboxInit() = 0
    PutS("  MailboxInit FAILED, MailboxError() = ")
    PutDec(MailboxError())
    PutNl()
    PutS("  (2 = the buffer landed at or above $40000000, where the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   VideoCore has no name for it. Build for a lower load addr.)")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn $BAD1
  EndIf
  RowAddr("mailbox buffer, ARM", MailboxBufferAddr())
  Row("mailbox buffer, bus", MailboxBusAddr())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  2. THE REFUSAL, PROVEN LIVE
  ;
  ;  Before the tags are filled, DisplayInit() must refuse. This is not
  ;  ceremony: the slot mechanism is the only thing standing between a
  ;  caller and a framebuffer configured with numbers nobody chose, and
  ;  a guard that has quietly stopped working looks exactly like a guard
  ;  that is working, right up until the day it matters.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 2. the refusal, before any tag is filled")
  PutNl()
  Chk("DisplayTagsReady() is 0", DisplayTagsReady(), 0)
  Chk("DisplayMissingSlot() names slot 0", DisplayMissingSlot(), #DSP_TAG_SETPHYS)
  rc = DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32)
  Chk("DisplayInit refuses with #DSP_ENOTAGS", rc, #DSP_ENOTAGS)
  Chk("DisplayReady() still 0", DisplayReady(), 0)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  3. FILL THE SLOTS
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 3. DisplayStandardTags()")
  PutNl()
  DisplayStandardTags()
  Chk("DisplayTagsReady() is 1", DisplayTagsReady(), 1)
  Chk("DisplayMissingSlot() is -1", DisplayMissingSlot(), -1)
  ; The numbers, printed, so the serial log carries the evidence and not
  ; just the claim. Compare against the header values quoted at the top
  ; of this file.
  Row("slot 0 set-physical", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETPHYS)
  Row("slot 1 set-virtual ", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETVIRT)
  Row("slot 2 set-depth   ", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETDEPTH)
  Row("slot 3 allocate    ", #MBX_TAG_FB_ALLOCATE)
  Row("slot 4 get-pitch   ", #MBX_TAG_FB_GETPITCH)
  Row("slot 5 set-order   ", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETORDER)
  Row("slot 6 set-offset  ", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETOFFSET)
  Row("slot 7 set-alpha   ", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETALPHA)
  Row("slot 8 set-overscan", #MBX_TAG_FB_SETOVERSCAN)
  Row("slot 9 get-physical", #MBX_TAG_FB_GETPHYS)

  ; THE SET-PIXEL-ORDER SLOT IS NOW FILLED, AND IT WAS AN ASSERTION HERE
  ; THAT IT MUST NOT BE. The reversal is written out because this file
  ; is where the original defect was caught and deleting the assertion
  ; without saying so would erase the only record of that.
  ;
  ; WHAT THE OLD CHECK GUARDED. The first DisplayStandardTags() filled
  ; slot 5, so DisplayInit() sent the tag carrying dsp_order - zero
  ; unless DisplayPixelOrder() had been called - and commanded the
  ; firmware's channel layout to a number nobody had chosen. The meaning
  ; of that number was not on this disk, so nobody COULD have chosen it
  ; responsibly. The first execution of this probe caught it.
  ;
  ; WHY IT IS RIGHT NOW, and both halves are needed:
  ;   * the value is cited - Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:373-374
  ;     gives BGR 0 and RGB 1, and the library sends BGR, which is the
  ;     arrangement DisplayRGB()'s packing already assumes and the one
  ;     the bench measured booting straight from the firmware.
  ;   * the nine tags now travel in ONE message and the firmware
  ;     allocates against what that message accumulated, so a tag left
  ;     out is a hole in that state rather than a default left alone.
  ;
  ; The second predicted failure at the top of this file has not gone
  ; away - only an eye settles whether the socket agrees with the word
  ; BGR - but it is now a disagreement with a request that can be
  ; printed, instead of with whatever the loader happened to leave.
  Chk("set-pixel-order slot IS filled", DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_SETORDER), 1)
  Chk("set-virtual-offset slot IS filled", DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_SETOFFSET), 1)
  Chk("set-alpha-mode slot IS filled", DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_SETALPHA), 1)
  Chk("set-overscan slot IS filled", DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_SETOVERSCAN), 1)
  Chk("get-physical slot IS filled", DisplayTagSet(#DSP_TAG_GETPHYS), 1)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4. DisplayInit - AND WHETHER HDMI HAS TO BE PLUGGED IN
  ;
  ;  The interesting reading with nothing connected. Three outcomes and
  ;  all three are results:
  ;    #DSP_OK, 1920x1080      - the firmware allocated the mode asked
  ;                              for and does not care about the cable
  ;    #DSP_OK, anything else  - it CLAMPED to a default; section 5 says
  ;                              to what
  ;    a negative code         - it refused; the code and MailboxError()
  ;                              say at which tag
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 4. DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32)")
  PutNl()
  rc = DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32)
  Row("return code", rc)
  Row("DisplayLastError()", DisplayLastError())
  Row("MailboxError()", MailboxError())
  If rc <> #DSP_OK
    Row("DisplayMissingSlot()", DisplayMissingSlot())
    PutS("  the return code names the step: -1 no tags, -2 bad args,")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  -3 bad depth, -4 no mailbox, -5 set-phys, -6 set-virt,")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  -7 set-depth, -8 set-order, -9 set-offset, -10 allocate,")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  -11 get-pitch, -12 zero base, -13 bad pitch, -14 short")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  size, -15 unusable geometry, -16 set-alpha, -17 set-overscan,")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  -18 the nine-tag buffer would not compose, -19 the message")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  went out and the transaction failed. MailboxError() 8")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  (#MBX_ERR_TAG) means the firmware did not recognise the tag")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  the code names; the transport reports WHICH tag of the nine.")
    PutNl()

    ; A CLAMP TO NOTHING IS NOT THE SAME AS A REFUSAL, so if 1080p was
    ; turned down, ask for a mode no display has ever failed to do. If
    ; THAT works the finding is "no HDMI means no big modes"; if it
    ; fails too the finding is "no HDMI means no framebuffer at all".
    ; Distinguishing those two is worth one more round trip.
    PutS("-- 4b. retrying at 640x480x32")
    PutNl()
    rc = DisplayInit(640, 480, 32)
    Row("return code", rc)
    Row("MailboxError()", MailboxError())
  EndIf

  If rc <> #DSP_OK
    PutS("=== no framebuffer. Nothing below can run. ===")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn $BAD2
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  5. EVERY NUMBER THE FIRMWARE CHOSE
  ;
  ;  These are the firmware's, not the request's. DisplayInit() carries
  ;  each tag's echo forward into the next step, so a clamped mode is
  ;  configured consistently instead of leaving the library's idea of
  ;  the geometry disagreeing with the firmware's.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 5. what the firmware actually gave back")
  PutNl()
  bus   = DisplayBusBase()
  base  = DisplayBase()
  size  = DisplaySize()
  w     = DisplayWidth()
  h     = DisplayHeight()
  depth = DisplayDepth()
  bpp   = DisplayBytesPerPixel()
  pitch = DisplayPitch()

  ; WHAT THE PANEL SAID BEFORE ANYTHING WAS SET, which is the input the
  ; combined message's set-physical was built from. A zero here means
  ; the query would not answer and the width and height this program
  ; passed in were used instead - a different experiment, and worth
  ; knowing which one was run.
  Row("panel width  (get-physical)", DisplayPanelWidth())
  Row("panel height (get-physical)", DisplayPanelHeight())

  Row("bus base (VideoCore)", bus)
  ; Bits 31:30 select which of the four aliases the VideoCore was using.
  ; $C0000000 is the one bcm2711.dtsi:45's dma-ranges describes, so this
  ; should read 3. Anything else means the & $3FFFFFFF below is being
  ; applied to an address it was not derived for, and DisplayBase() is
  ; then a plausible wrong number rather than an obviously wrong one.
  Row("bus alias selector (bits 31:30)", (bus >> 30) & 3)
  RowAddr("ARM base (bus & $3FFFFFFF)", base)
  Row("allocated size, bytes", size)
  Row("width  (CLAMPED value)", w)
  Row("height (CLAMPED value)", h)
  Row("depth  (CLAMPED value)", depth)
  Row("bytes per pixel", bpp)
  Row("PITCH, bytes per row", pitch)

  tight = w * bpp
  pad = pitch - tight
  Row("width * bpp (NOT the pitch)", tight)
  Row("padding per row, bytes", pad)
  Row("rows the size covers", size / pitch)

  Chk("size covers pitch*height", size >= (pitch * h), 1)
  Chk("pitch is at least width*bpp", pitch >= tight, 1)
  Chk("bus alias selector is 3", (bus >> 30) & 3, 3)
  Chk("ARM base is bus minus the alias", base, bus & $3FFFFFFF)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  6. IS IT SAFE TO WRITE?
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 6. is the surface somewhere safe to write")
  PutNl()
  If PrRegionSafe(base, size) = 0
    PutS("  REFUSING TO DRAW. The surface is at $")
    PutHex(base, 16)
    PutS(" for ")
    PutDec(size)
    PutS(" bytes,")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  which is not clear of the low 64 MiB where this image, its")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  stack and the monitor live. Writing there would overwrite")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  the running program, not the screen.")
    PutNl()
    prFails = prFails + 1
    ProcedureReturn $BAD3
  EndIf
  PutS("  ok   surface is clear of the low 64 MiB")
  PutNl()

  If w < #MIN_W Or h < #MIN_H
    PutS("  the mode is too small to lay a picture out in; stopping")
    PutS(" here rather than reporting failures that are about the mode.")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn prFails
  EndIf

  black = DisplayRGB(0, 0, 0)
  red   = DisplayRGB(255, 0, 0)
  green = DisplayRGB(0, 255, 0)
  blue  = DisplayRGB(0, 0, 255)
  white = DisplayRGB(255, 255, 255)
  ; A dark blue field rather than black, so that "cleared" and "never
  ; written" are different sights on a monitor and different numbers in
  ; a readback.
  field = DisplayRGB(0, 0, 40)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  7. THE PITCH, PROVEN AGAINST THE REAL NUMBER
  ;
  ;  THIS IS THE SECTION THAT DOES NOT NEED A MONITOR AND IS THE WHOLE
  ;  REASON THIS PROBE IS WORTH RUNNING TODAY.
  ;
  ;  Clear the surface, write single pixels through display.pi4, then
  ;  read the raw bytes back at addresses computed HERE from the pitch
  ;  the firmware reported. Two claims:
  ;
  ;    a) a pixel written at row y is at base + y*pitch  -> it is there
  ;    b) it is NOT at base + y*width*bpp                -> the shear
  ;
  ;  (b) is only a real control when pitch differs from width*bpp. When
  ;  they are equal the two addresses ARE the same address and the check
  ;  says nothing; it is reported as VACUOUS rather than as a pass,
  ;  because a check that cannot fail is not evidence.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 7. pitch arithmetic, read back from DRAM")
  PutNl()
  DisplayClear(black)

  ; $00ABCDEF has its top byte CLEAR, which no DisplayRGB() colour ever
  ; does, so it cannot be confused with the background or with any
  ; rectangle drawn later.
  magic = $00ABCDEF
  DisplayPixel(0, 1, magic)
  Chk("pixel(0,1) is at base + 1*pitch", PrRaw32(base, pitch, 0, 1), magic)
  If pad = 0
    PutS("  --   the width*bpp control is VACUOUS: this mode's pitch")
    PutNl()
    PutS("       equals width*bpp, so both addresses are the same one.")
    PutNl()
  Else
    Chk("the pixel is NOT at base + 1*width*bpp (the shear)", PrIsNot(PrRaw32(base, tight, 0, 1), magic), 1)
  EndIf

  ; The same claim further out, because an off-by-one in the row
  ; multiply is invisible at row 1 if the code happens to use y-1 - and
  ; because at row 1 the tight-stride address lands in row 0's padding,
  ; where nothing was ever written; by row 7 it lands in a real pixel of
  ; row 6, so this is the version of the control that has to reason
  ; about a written surface rather than an untouched one.
  DisplayPixel(3, 7, magic)
  Chk("pixel(3,7) is at base + 7*pitch + 3*4", PrRaw32(base, pitch, 3, 7), magic)
  If pad <> 0
    Chk("NOT at base + 7*width*bpp + 3*4", PrIsNot(PrRaw32(base, tight, 3, 7), magic), 1)
    ; The padding itself. Every row has `pad` bytes past its last pixel
    ; that are not part of the picture, and a library addressing rows as
    ; y*width*bpp would put row y+1 into them. Row 0's is checked here
    ; because it is the one a clear has already had every chance to
    ; scribble on.
    Chk("row 0's padding is untouched", PeekN(base + tight) & $FFFFFFFF, 0)
    Chk("row 1's padding is untouched", PeekN(base + pitch + tight) & $FFFFFFFF, 0)
  EndIf

  ; Along a row the stride is bpp and not the pitch.
  DisplayPixel(1, 0, magic)
  Chk("pixel(1,0) is at base + 1*4", PrRaw32(base, pitch, 1, 0), magic)

  ; The LAST pixel of a row and the FIRST of the next. If row addressing
  ; were tight-packed these two would be adjacent words; with a padded
  ; pitch they are `pad` bytes apart, and writing one must not disturb
  ; the other.
  DisplayPixel(w - 1, 5, red)
  DisplayPixel(0, 6, blue)
  Chk("last pixel of row 5 held", PrRaw32(base, pitch, w - 1, 5), red)
  Chk("first pixel of row 6 held", PrRaw32(base, pitch, 0, 6), blue)

  ; Clipping. A write one past the right edge must land nowhere - and
  ; "nowhere" specifically must not be the first pixel of the next row,
  ; which is exactly where a missing clip puts it.
  ;
  ; The expected value is BLACK and not zero: the surface was cleared to
  ; DisplayRGB(0,0,0), which is $FF000000 because the top byte is set.
  DisplayPixel(w, 8, white)
  Chk("write at x=width did not land on row 9", PrRaw32(base, pitch, 0, 9), black)
  If pad <> 0
    ; Where an unclipped write would ACTUALLY go if the clip were the
    ; only thing missing: straight into row 8's padding.
    Chk("write at x=width did not land in row 8's padding", PeekN(base + (8 * pitch) + tight) & $FFFFFFFF, 0)
  EndIf
  Chk("DisplayGetPixel off-surface is -1", DisplayGetPixel(w, 8), -1)
  ; ...and DisplayGetPixel agrees with the raw read where it is defined.
  ; This is the one place the library's own reader is the thing tested.
  Chk("DisplayGetPixel agrees with the raw read", DisplayGetPixel(3, 7), magic)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  8. CACHE MAINTENANCE - VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED
  ;
  ;  Under the monitor the MMU and caches are ON, so pixels written and
  ;  not cleaned out of the D-cache never reach the memory the display
  ;  controller scans. display.pi4 auto-flushes by default. The claim
  ;  under test is that the dc cvac loop is REALLY RUNNING.
  ;
  ;  READ BACK CANNOT SHOW IT, and this is worth being explicit about
  ;  because section 7 looks like it should: a read hits the same dirty
  ;  cache line and returns the right answer whether or not the line was
  ;  ever cleaned. Section 7 passing says nothing at all about the
  ;  cache. The only thing that distinguishes a flush that happened from
  ;  one that did not is that it COST something.
  ;
  ;  So, timed on CNTPCT_EL0 through MbxTicks():
  ;    A  a full-surface fill, auto-flush OFF   - pixels only
  ;    B  DisplayFlush() on its own             - cleaning only
  ;    C  a full-surface fill, auto-flush ON    - both
  ;
  ;  B must be greater than zero: a flush of the whole surface is
  ;  size/64 cache lines and that cannot take no time. B at or near zero
  ;  means the maintenance is not happening whatever the source says.
  ;  C must exceed A: if it matches A instead, the auto-flush path is
  ;  not reaching the code the explicit flush reaches.
  ;
  ;  WHAT THIS STILL DOES NOT PROVE: that dc cvac is the RIGHT
  ;  maintenance operation for this reader. Only a monitor showing
  ;  fresh pixels can settle that. It proves the instructions execute.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 8. cache maintenance, measured")
  PutNl()
  Row("CNTFRQ_EL0, Hz", MbxCounterHz())

  DisplayAutoFlush(0)
  t0 = MbxTicks()
  DisplayClear(black)
  t1 = MbxTicks()
  DisplayFlush()
  t2 = MbxTicks()
  DisplayAutoFlush(1)
  DisplayClear(black)
  t3 = MbxTicks()

  Row("A  fill, no flush   (ticks)", t1 - t0)
  Row("B  flush alone      (ticks)", t2 - t1)
  Row("C  fill + autoflush (ticks)", t3 - t2)
  Row("cache lines B had to clean", size / 64)
  Chk("B > 0: the dc cvac loop really ran", (t2 - t1) > 0, 1)
  Chk("C > A: auto-flush costs what flushing costs", (t3 - t2) > (t1 - t0), 1)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  9. THE PICTURE
  ;
  ;  Known colours at computed coordinates, all of them printed. Reading
  ;  the rectangles back proves they were written where they were asked
  ;  for; it says NOTHING about what a screen would show. The printed
  ;  pixel words are for whoever plugs a monitor in: red appearing where
  ;  the log says blue was written is the channel swap, and
  ;  DisplayColourSwap(1) is the one-line fix.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 9. drawing")
  PutNl()
  Row("pixel word for pure RED  ", red)
  Row("pixel word for pure GREEN", green)
  Row("pixel word for pure BLUE ", blue)
  Row("pixel word for WHITE     ", white)
  Row("pixel word for the FIELD ", field)

  ; The layout, in twelfths of the surface. Three rectangles across the
  ; top with a gap between them, one below the first, a frame round the
  ; lot, and the console under it. rx + 3*rw + 2*gap is 2/3 of the
  ; width, so it fits at any mode this section runs at.
  rx  = w / 12
  ry  = h / 12
  rw  = w / 6
  rh  = h / 8
  gap = rw / 4
  Row("rect x", rx)
  Row("rect y", ry)
  Row("rect w", rw)
  Row("rect h", rh)
  Row("rect gap", gap)

  DisplayClear(field)
  DisplayFillRect(rx, ry, rw, rh, red)
  DisplayFillRect(rx + rw + gap, ry, rw, rh, green)
  DisplayFillRect(rx + (2 * (rw + gap)), ry, rw, rh, blue)
  DisplayFillRect(rx, ry + rh + gap, rw, rh, white)
  DisplayFrameRect(rx - 2, ry - 2, (3 * rw) + (2 * gap) + 4, (2 * rh) + gap + 4, white)

  ; Read the middle of each back. Centre and not a corner, so an
  ; off-by-one in the fill's clip cannot make the check pass on a
  ; rectangle that is one pixel short.
  Chk("red rect centre",   PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx + (rw / 2), ry + (rh / 2)), red)
  Chk("green rect centre", PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx + rw + gap + (rw / 2), ry + (rh / 2)), green)
  Chk("blue rect centre",  PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx + (2 * (rw + gap)) + (rw / 2), ry + (rh / 2)), blue)
  Chk("white rect centre", PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx + (rw / 2), ry + rh + gap + (rh / 2)), white)
  ; ...and that the fill stopped where it was told to. The pixel at
  ; rx+rw-1 is the last one inside; rx+rw is the first one outside.
  Chk("red rect ends at rx+rw-1", PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx + rw - 1, ry + (rh / 2)), red)
  Chk("red rect does not reach rx+rw", PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx + rw, ry + (rh / 2)), field)
  Chk("frame top-left corner", PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx - 2, ry - 2), white)
  ; The frame is ONE pixel thick, so the pixel diagonally inside its
  ; corner must still be the field. A frame drawn as a filled rectangle
  ; would pass every check above and fail this one.
  Chk("frame is one pixel thick", PrRaw32(base, pitch, rx - 1, ry - 1), field)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  10. TEXT
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 10. text")
  PutNl()
  cw  = DisplayCharWidth()
  chh = DisplayCharHeight()
  Row("cell width, pixels", cw)
  Row("cell height, pixels", chh)

  ; THE GLYPH, CHECKED AGAINST ITS OWN TABLE. Drawn in the bottom-right
  ; corner, well clear of the console, so the console's text cannot
  ; supply a false pass. 'H' is used because its top row is two separate
  ; stems and its middle row is solid - a cell that renders one and not
  ; the other is caught - but the comparison is over all 128 pixels, so
  ; the choice of character is convenience rather than the method.
  gx = w - cw - 1
  gy = h - chh - 1
  DisplayFillRect(gx, gy, cw, chh, black)
  DisplayChar(gx, gy, 72, white, black)
  Row("glyph cell x", gx)
  Row("glyph cell y", gy)
  Chk("all 128 pixels of 'H' match the font table", PrGlyphErrors(base, pitch, gx, gy, 72, white, black), 0)

  ; THE CONSOLE. Under the picture, using what is left of the surface.
  conx = rx
  cony = ry + (2 * rh) + gap + 8
  conw = w - (2 * rx)
  conh = h - cony - ry
  Row("console x", conx)
  Row("console y", cony)
  Row("console w", conw)
  Row("console h", conh)
  If DisplayConsole(conx, cony, conw, conh) = 0
    PutS("  DisplayConsole refused - no whole cell fits in that window")
    PutNl()
    prFails = prFails + 1
  Else
    Row("console columns", DisplayCols())
    Row("console rows", DisplayRows())
    DisplayColour(white, field)
    DisplayCls()

    ; ONE SHORT LINE FIRST, and the ink counted before anything can
    ; wrap or scroll it away. A count taken after four long lines on a
    ; three-row console would be measuring whatever survived the scroll.
    DisplayPrintLn("PureMetal Forge")
    ncell = 10
    If ncell > DisplayCols()
      ncell = DisplayCols()
    EndIf
    ink = PrInkCount(base, pitch, conx, cony, cw * ncell, chh, field)
    Row("cells counted", ncell)
    Row("ink pixels in them", ink)
    Chk("the text rendered something", ink > 0, 1)
    ; ...and did not render EVERYTHING, which is what a glyph lookup
    ; reading past the end of the table into all-ones would do.
    Chk("the text is not a solid block", ink < (cw * ncell * chh), 1)

    ; Then the rest, which exercises wrap, scroll and the number
    ; formatters. Nothing is asserted about it; it is there so that a
    ; monitor, when one is plugged in, has something worth reading.
    DisplayPrint("mode ")
    DisplayPrintDec(w)
    DisplayPrint("x")
    DisplayPrintDec(h)
    DisplayPrint("x")
    DisplayPrintDec(depth)
    DisplayNewLine()
    DisplayPrint("pitch ")
    DisplayPrintDec(pitch)
    DisplayPrint(" base $")
    DisplayPrintHex(base, 8)
    DisplayNewLine()
    DisplayPrintLn("ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ 0123456789")
    DisplayPrintLn("the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog")
    Chk("the cursor is on the last console row", DisplayCursorRow(), DisplayRows() - 1)
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE VERDICT
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutNl()
  PutS("=== ")
  PutDec(prChecks - prFails)
  PutS(" of ")
  PutDec(prChecks)
  PutS(" checks passed ===")
  PutNl()
  PutS("NEEDED NO MONITOR: the mode numbers, the pitch, the bus-to-ARM")
  PutNl()
  PutS("conversion, every readback, the clipping, the glyph comparison")
  PutNl()
  PutS("and the cache timing.")
  PutNl()
  PutS("STILL NEEDS ONE PLUGGED IN: whether a picture appears at all,")
  PutNl()
  PutS("whether it leans (that is the pitch), whether dc cvac is the")
  PutNl()
  PutS("right maintenance op for this reader, and whether the LEFT-HAND")
  PutNl()
  PutS("rectangle looks RED - if it looks blue, the channels are")
  PutNl()
  PutS("swapped and DisplayColourSwap(1) fixes it.")
  PutNl()

  ProcedureReturn prFails
EndProcedure
